@file Compfiles/UK2024R1P4.lean
@decl UK2024R1P4.sq_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Key inequality (*): if n·2ⁿ + 1 is a perfect square and n ≥ 2, then 2^(n-1) ≤ 2n + 2
-- L4DEL: intuition: Key inequality (*): if n·2ⁿ + 1 is a perfect square and n ≥ 2, then 2^(n-1) ≤ 2n + 2. Strategy: factor n·2ⁿ = (k-1)(k+1). Since n·2ⁿ is even, k² = n·2ⁿ+1 is odd, so k is odd — write k = 2a+1. Then (k-1)(k+1) = 4a(a+1), so a(a+1) = n·2^(n-2). The integers a and a+1 are coprime with exactly one even, so the full factor 2^(n-2) lands on that even one.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/UK2024R1P4.lean
@decl UK2024R1P4.two_pow_sub_two_gt
-- L4DEL: title: For n ≥ 5, n + 1 < 2^(n-2)
-- L4DEL: intuition: For n ≥ 5, n + 1 < 2^(n-2).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

