-- L4DEL: intuition: The prime `p` occurs with multiplicity exactly one in a product of three pairwise distinct primes `p`, `q`, `r`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa1973P5.lean
@decl Usa1973P5.key_algebra
-- L4DEL: title: The algebraic heart of the argument
-- L4DEL: intuition: The algebraic heart of the argument. If `∛q = ∛p + m d` and `∛r = ∛p + n d`, then `n ∛q - m ∛r = (n - m) ∛p`; cubing that relation and regrouping expresses `3 m n (n - m) ∛p ∛q ∛r` as an integer polynomial in `p, q, r`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa1973P5.lean
@decl Usa1973P5.not_cube_mul_cube
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: not_cube_mul_cube
-- L4DEL: intuition: A product of three pairwise distinct primes times a nonzero cube is never a cube: comparing the exponent of `p` in the prime factorizations of both sides of `p * q * r * b ^ 3 = a ^ 3` would give `1 + 3k = 3j`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa1973P5.lean
@decl Usa1973P5.usa1973_p5
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: usa1973_p5
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `∛p, ∛q, ∛r` are terms of an arithmetic progression with common difference `d`, at integer positions differing by `m` and `n` from the position of `∛p`, then `∛q = ∛p + m d` and `∛r = ∛p + n d` as below, so it suffices to show that this is impossible for distinct primes `p, q, r`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own leading source comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

