@file Compfiles/Usa1995P2.lean
@decl Usa1995P2.reachable_sqrt_div
-- L4DEL: title: Every number of the form `√(m / n)` with `m, n` positive naturals can be reached from `0` by a finite sequence of trigonometric maps
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every number of the form `√(m / n)` with `m, n` positive naturals can be reached from `0` by a finite sequence of trigonometric maps. Proof by well-founded induction on `m + n` (Euclidean algorithm): - if `m = n` then `√(m/n) = 1 = cos 0`; - if `m > n` then `√(m/n) = √(1 + (√((m-n)/n))²)` and `(m-n) + n < m + n`; - if `m < n` then `√(m/n) = 1 / √(n/m)` and `√(n/m) = √(1 + (√((n-m)/m))²)` with `(n-m) + m < m + n`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa1995P2.lean
@decl Usa1995P2.run
-- L4DEL: title: Apply a finite sequence of trigonometric maps to a starting value
-- L4DEL: intuition: Apply a finite sequence of trigonometric maps to a starting value.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa1995P2.lean
@decl Usa1995P2.run_chainInv
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: run_chainInv
-- L4DEL: intuition: The chain `arctan, sin, arccos, tan` sends `x` to `1 / x`: with `t = arctan x` we have `sin t = x / √(1 + x²)`, and if `u = arccos (sin t)` then `tan u = sin u / cos u = √(1 - x²/(1+x²)) / (x/√(1+x²)) = 1 / x`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

