@file Compfiles/Usa2006P3.lean
@decl Usa2006P3.exists_root_of_pbound
-- L4DEL: title: If `f` is nonconstant and satisfies the prime bound, then `f` has a root of the form `(2k+1)²/4` over `ℚ`
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `f` is nonconstant and satisfies the prime bound, then `f` has a root of the form `(2k+1)²/4` over `ℚ`. This is the heart of the proof: a large prime `q` dividing `f(n²)` forces `q = 2n + 2k + 1` with `k ≤ B`, and then `q` divides `Fs f (2k+1)`; since there are infinitely many such primes but only finitely many possible `k`, some `Fs f (2k+1)` vanishes.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

