@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
@decl Usa2019P6.comp_neg_X_of_phiPoly_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: title: `P` is even: from `PhiPoly P = 0` we get `Qfun P t 0 (-t) = 0` for all real `t`, hence `t * (P(t) − P(−t)) = 0` for all `t`, so `P.comp (-X) = P`
-- L4DEL: intuition: `P` is even: from `PhiPoly P = 0` we get `Qfun P t 0 (-t) = 0` for all real `t`, hence `t * (P(t) − P(−t)) = 0` for all `t`, so `P.comp (-X) = P`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
@decl Usa2019P6.Fk_coeff
-- L4DEL: title: The leading coefficient of the second finite difference `(X+h)^k + (X−h)^k − 2X^k` at degree `k−2` is `k * (k−1) * h²`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The leading coefficient of the second finite difference `(X+h)^k + (X−h)^k − 2X^k` at degree `k−2` is `k * (k−1) * h²`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
@decl Usa2019P6.Fk_natDegree_le
-- L4DEL: title: The second finite difference `(X+h)^k + (X−h)^k − 2X^k` has degree at most `k−2` (in natural subtraction)
-- L4DEL: intuition: The second finite difference `(X+h)^k + (X−h)^k − 2X^k` has degree at most `k−2` (in natural subtraction). Expanded via the binomial theorem: the top two terms cancel.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
@decl Usa2019P6.natDegree_le_two
-- L4DEL: title: The degree bound: if `PhiPoly P = 0` and `P` is even, then `P.natDegree ≤ 2`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The degree bound: if `PhiPoly P = 0` and `P` is even, then `P.natDegree ≤ 2`. The proof passes to complex polynomials and evaluates `Qfun` at the surface point `(x, h, -h)` with `h² = −1/2`, obtaining that the second finite difference of `P` at `h` is constant; comparing leading coefficients then forces `deg P ≤ 2`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
@decl Usa2019P6.PhiPoly
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: PhiPoly
-- L4DEL: intuition: The "cleared denominator" polynomial: for `n = P.natDegree`, the rational function `(x, y) ↦ (2xy−1)^(n+1) * Qfun P x y ((x+y)/(2xy−1))` is actually a polynomial function, and this is the corresponding polynomial, written as a polynomial in `y` whose coefficients are polynomials in `x`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
@decl Usa2019P6.phiPoly_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: title: If `Qfun P` vanishes at every nonzero real triple on the surface `2xyz = x+y+z`, then `PhiPoly P` is the zero polynomial
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `Qfun P` vanishes at every nonzero real triple on the surface `2xyz = x+y+z`, then `PhiPoly P` is the zero polynomial. This is the "real dimension two" argument: a bivariate polynomial vanishing on a cofinite union of points is zero.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
@decl Usa2019P6.phiPoly_eval
-- L4DEL: title: Evaluating `PhiPoly` at a pair `(x, y)` with `2xy ≠ 1` gives the cleared value of `Qfun` at the surface point `(x, y, (x+y)/(2xy−1))`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Evaluating `PhiPoly` at a pair `(x, y)` with `2xy ≠ 1` gives the cleared value of `Qfun` at the surface point `(x, y, (x+y)/(2xy−1))`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2019P6.lean
