@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.Good
-- L4DEL: title: The property required of `n`: writing the divisors of `n !` in increasing order as `1 = d₁ < d₂ < ⋯ < dₖ = n !`, the consecutive gaps are non-decreasing
-- L4DEL: intuition: The property required of `n`: writing the divisors of `n !` in increasing order as `1 = d₁ < d₂ < ⋯ < dₖ = n !`, the consecutive gaps are non-decreasing. We phrase this using consecutive triples: whenever `a < b < c` are divisors of `n !` such that no divisor of `n !` lies strictly between `a` and `b`, or strictly between `b` and `c`, we have `b - a ≤ c - b`. (Note: we write `Nat.factorial n` instead of `n !` since the `!` notation clashes with `PiLp.vecNotation` under a full `Mathlib` import.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.good_four
-- L4DEL: title: The divisors of `4 !
-- L4DEL: intuition: The divisors of `4 ! = 24` are `1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24`, with gaps `1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 12`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.good_three
-- L4DEL: title: The divisors of `3 !
-- L4DEL: intuition: The divisors of `3 ! = 6` are `1, 2, 3, 6`, with gaps `1, 1, 3`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.mul_dvd_factorial_of_ne
-- L4DEL: title: If `a` and `b` are distinct positive integers with `a, b ≤ n`, then `a * b ∣ Nat.factorial n`: both occur as distinct factors in `n !
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `a` and `b` are distinct positive integers with `a, b ≤ n`, then `a * b ∣ Nat.factorial n`: both occur as distinct factors in `n ! = ∏ i ∈ Finset.range n, (i + 1)`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.not_good_five
-- L4DEL: title: `n = 5` fails: `15, 20, 24` are consecutive divisors of `5 !
-- L4DEL: intuition: `n = 5` fails: `15, 20, 24` are consecutive divisors of `5 ! = 120`, but `20 - 15 = 5 > 4 = 24 - 20`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.not_good_of_seven_le
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: not_good_of_seven_le
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every `n` with `7 ≤ n ≤ 12` fails: `12, 14, 15` are consecutive divisors of `n !` (there is no divisor strictly between `12` and `14` since `13 ∤ n !`), but `14 - 12 = 2 > 1 = 15 - 14`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.not_good_of_thirteen_le
-- L4DEL: title: Every `n ≥ 13` fails
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every `n ≥ 13` fails. With `m = ⌊n / 2⌋ ≥ 6`, both `m² - 1` and `m²` divide `n !`, hence are consecutive divisors of `n !` with gap `1`. A downward induction using `Good n` then shows that every integer in `[1, m²]` divides `n !`, which contradicts Bertrand's postulate: there is a prime `p` with `n < p ≤ 2n ≤ m²`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2024P1.lean
@decl Usa2024P1.not_good_six
-- L4DEL: title: `n = 6` fails: `90, 120, 144` are consecutive divisors of `6 !
-- L4DEL: intuition: `n = 6` fails: `90, 120, 144` are consecutive divisors of `6 ! = 720`, but `120 - 90 = 30 > 24 = 144 - 120`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

