@file Compfiles/Usa2025P5.lean
@decl Usa2025P5.choose_mul_prod_filter_eq
-- L4DEL: title: Splitting `n.choose i * (i)!
-- L4DEL: intuition: Splitting `n.choose i * (i)! = ∏ j ∈ range i, (n - j)` into the factors with `p ∣ j + 1` and those with `p ∤ j + 1`, and cancelling the common factor `p ^ (i / p) * (i / p)!`, gives `n.choose i * P = (M - 1).choose (i / p) * R`, where `P = ∏ j, (j + 1)` and `R = ∏ j, (n - j)` are the products over the `j < i` with `p ∤ j + 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file Compfiles/Usa2025P5.lean
@decl Usa2025P5.dvd_sum_of_even
-- L4DEL: title: Sufficiency: for even `k`, `n + 1` divides `∑ i ∈ range (n + 1), (n.choose i) ^ k` for every positive `n`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sufficiency: for even `k`, `n + 1` divides `∑ i ∈ range (n + 1), (n.choose i) ^ k` for every positive `n`. Proof by strong induction on `n`: for each prime `p` with `p ^ e` exactly dividing `n + 1`, `sum_modEq` reduces the claim modulo `p ^ e` to the induction hypothesis at `M - 1`, where `p * M = n + 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

